{
  "requirement": "Each configurable feature MUST define the name and typed data structure of its\nuser section and any supported project section, citing these common configuration\nrequirements. The feature MUST specify its supported keys, value types, defaults,\nvalidation rules, and the precedence or combination rules for user settings,\nproject settings, and invocation options. It MUST specify the behavior of an\nabsent section and which operations require that section.\n\nFeature-specific fields MUST stay within the feature's section. A feature MUST\nNOT reinterpret another feature's settings or change the shared file locations\nor schema version. A feature MAY define a provider-owned options map, but MUST\ndocument that boundary and how those options are validated.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: false.** The authoring chapter satisfies the obligation in substance. The gap is that nothing is marked as implementing or testing it, and no FIXME acknowledges that.\n\n**Substance.** `feature_sections` (md/requirements/configuration.md, \"Feature sections\") sets obligations for each configurable feature. The only configurable feature today is evidence authoring (md/requirements/authoring.md, \"Configuration\"), and its text carries out those obligations:\n- **Section names and typed structures:** `configuration_structure` defines `UserAuthoringConfig`, `WorkspaceAuthoringConfig`, `AuthoringConfig`, the backend configs and `OptionValue`. `configuration_serialization` maps them to `[authoring]`, `[authoring.configs.<name>]` and `projects`. The `configuration` requirement cites `user_location`, `structure`, `feature_sections`, `workspace_location` and `workspace_structure`.\n- **Keys, types, defaults, validation:** `concurrency` gives the defaults (CPU count for Bedrock, one for Ollama) and rejects zero. `configuration_storage` covers name syntax, model ID, `api_key_env` and project-root rules. `credentials` forbids combining `credentials` with `aws_profile`, and `api_key` with `api_key_env`.\n- **Precedence and combination:** `configuration_precedence` orders `--config`, then the per-project user selection, then the workspace default, then the user default. Workspace records replace same-named user records as a whole, with no field merging.\n- **Absent section:** workspace-only authoring works without a user file, and a missing configuration or unknown selection must name the file and point to examples. Ordinary validation does not require authoring settings (`authoring.deterministic_validation`, `configuration.loading`).\n- **Provider-owned options:** the `options` map boundary and its validation are documented in `configuration_storage`. src/config.rs (`BackendConfig::validate`) enforces it by rejecting model, prompt and tool keys.\n- **Scope limits:** the common root accepts only `version`, `authoring` and `evidence` (`validate_workspace_common`).\n\n**Marking gap.**\n- `references` shows only two `{cite}` sites for this requirement: md/requirements/authoring.md:486 (the `configuration` requirement) and md/requirements/configuration.md:108 (`workspace_structure`). There is no `{impl}` and no `{test}`.\n- Under `requirements.references.targets` and `requirements.ci.review_criteria`, `{cite}` only means \"relies on or refers to\". A requirement can implement part of another via `{impl}`. The authoring requirements (`configuration_structure`, `configuration_serialization`, `configuration_storage`, `configuration_precedence`, `concurrency`, `credentials`) carry out parts of this obligation but only reference it indirectly. The `configuration` requirement only cites it. The review criteria treat a cite-only reference as an unmarked implementation, which is a gap.\n- The Rust code that enforces section scoping and validation is not marked either. These are `validate_workspace_common`, `WorkspaceConfig::validate`, `UserConfig::validate` and `resolve_authoring_with_workspace_from`. They carry `{impl}` markers for the more specific requirements, but none for `feature_sections`.\n\n**Tests.** No `{test}` references this requirement. tests/configuration.rs and tests/evidence_boundaries.rs do check related behavior: unknown root keys are rejected, absent and minimal configs are handled, and precedence is applied. They are attached to other requirements, though, and nothing tests the feature-section obligations directly. That includes the options-map boundary, which only `invalid_schema_and_feature_values_report_the_configuration_path` exercises. The obligation is mostly a specification-time rule, so extensive tests might be impractical. The absence of any `{test}` or `{impl}` marking is still unexplained in the sources.\n\n**FIXME.** No FIXME or other acknowledgment of these gaps appears in the sources, so `FIXME` is not available.",
